const GLOSSARY = [
['Hammer price','молотковая цена','The price at which the auctioneer’s gavel falls; the buyer’s premium is added on top. Press reports quote hammer-plus-premium unless stated.'],
['Buyer’s premium','премия покупателя','The house’s fee charged to the buyer, now 28–30% at the lower tier (2026) and tiered down to ≈14–15% above $8m. It is where most auction-house revenue sits.'],
['Estimate','эстимейт','The pre-sale low–high range set by the specialist; the low estimate normally sits just above the reserve. Lots “sold within estimate” are the market’s baseline.'],
['Reserve','резервная цена','The confidential minimum below which the house will not sell; never above the low estimate. A lot that fails to reach it is bought in.'],
['Bought in (BI)','не продан','Unsold at auction. A bought-in trophy can be “burned” for several years because the market knows the failed price.'],
['Guarantee','гарантия','A minimum price the house promises the consignor regardless of the result; the house takes the risk or lays it off to a third party.'],
['Third-party guarantee / irrevocable bid','безотзывная ставка','An outside party commits to bid a set amount before the sale in exchange for a share of the upside (the “financing fee”). Disclosed in catalogues by a symbol.'],
['Chandelier bidding','ставки «с люстры»','Bids called out by the auctioneer below the reserve to open the lot; legal, and signalled in conditions of sale.'],
['Fair warning','последнее предупреждение','The auctioneer’s notice that the hammer is about to fall.'],
['Sell-through rate','доля проданных лотов','Percentage of lots sold; 85–91% is a strong evening sale, below 75% is a weak one.'],
['White-glove sale','продажа «в белых перчатках»','Every lot sold. ArtTactic counted 131 of them in H1 2026.'],
['Consignment','передача на комиссию','The agreement under which the seller hands a work to a house or dealer for sale; the seller’s commission (often waived for trophies) is set here.'],
['Seller’s commission','комиссия продавца','Fee charged to the consignor; negotiable to zero (or an “enhanced hammer”) for the most desirable property.'],
['Private sale / private treaty','частная продажа','A sale brokered by the house or a dealer outside the auction room at a fixed or negotiated price. Christie’s passed $1bn and Sotheby’s $826m in H1 2026.'],
['Fresh to market','впервые на рынке','Not offered publicly for decades; the single strongest driver of a premium (the Blaquier-Arrieta works were unseen for ≈50 years).'],
['Single-owner sale','аукцион одной коллекции','A sale devoted to one collection, marketed as a brand (Allen $1.6bn, Newhouse $631m, Lewis $393m). Estates are the growth engine of 2025–26.'],
['Provenance','провенанс','The chain of ownership from the artist to today. Gaps between 1933 and 1945 trigger restitution checks; dealer stock numbers and sale lots are the evidence.'],
['Catalogue raisonné','каталог-резоне','The scholarly list of an artist’s authentic works; inclusion is the practical test of authenticity for most modern artists.'],
['Condition report','отчёт о состоянии','The conservator’s or specialist’s account of a work’s physical state, requested before bidding.'],
['Due diligence','due diligence, комплексная проверка','Title, authenticity, provenance, sanctions and export checks before a sale closes; registries include the Art Loss Register and Interpol.'],
['Title','право собственности','Legal ownership. Art is a “buyer-beware” market: the buyer must satisfy itself on title and authenticity.'],
['Droit de suite (ARR)','право следования','Artist’s resale royalty in the EU and UK (capped), paid on secondary sales of living artists and estates under copyright.'],
['Freeport','фрипорт','Bonded warehouse where goods are stored and traded without import duty or sales tax (Geneva, Singapore, Delaware).'],
['Art-secured lending','кредит под залог искусства','Loans at ≈50% loan-to-value against blue-chip works; Sotheby’s Financial Services’ book exceeds $1.8bn.'],
['Flipping','перепродажа','Reselling a recent primary purchase quickly at auction; galleries police it with resale clauses and blacklists.'],
['Primary vs secondary market','первичный и вторичный рынок','Primary: first sale, usually by the artist’s gallery. Secondary: any resale — auctions and dealers.'],
['Specialist','специалист','Department expert who sources, values and catalogues; the core auction-house career track.'],
['Business getter','«добытчик»','A senior figure whose job is winning consignments through relationships, not scholarship.'],
['Evening sale / day sale','вечерние и дневные торги','Evening sales carry the trophies and the guarantees; day sales carry the volume and set the mood of the middle market.'],
['Marquee week','главная неделя торгов','The New York weeks in May and November when all three houses sell their best material within days.'],
['Vetting','экспертиза ярмарки','Independent committees at TEFAF and Art Basel that inspect every object for authenticity and condition before the fair opens.'],
['Priority bidding','приоритетная ставка','Phillips’ 2025 format: a binding bid lodged before the sale in return for a lower premium.'],
['Sealed-bid auction','закрытый аукцион','Bidders submit one confidential bid; Lévy Gorvy Dayan’s “LGD Hammer” adapted it for single trophy lots in 2026.'],
['Burned','«сгоревший» лот','A work that failed publicly and is hard to resell until the market forgets.'],
['Rostrum','трибуна аукциониста','The auctioneer’s podium; the working name of this tool before it became Subhasta.'],
['SIC code','код отрасли','Standard Industrial Classification; the UK is adding 47.921 so that auction sales can finally be measured as a sector.']
];

const ANATOMY = [
['Sourcing','A business getter or specialist wins the consignment from an estate, a collector or a dealer; the “three Ds” (death, divorce, debt) supply most property.'],
['Valuation and estimate','Comparables from the price databases, the catalogue raisonné and the condition report produce a low–high estimate; the reserve is set at or below the low estimate.'],
['Contract','Consignment agreement: seller’s commission (often waived for trophies), reserve, any guarantee and whether the house lays it off to a third party, marketing budget, insurance and shipping.'],
['Cataloguing','Provenance research, literature, exhibition history, essay; the catalogue note is the house’s public case for the price.'],
['Marketing and tour','Trophies travel to Hong Kong, London, Paris and the Gulf for private previews with named clients; press announcements set the narrative.'],
['Pre-sale interest','Specialists call clients and lodge absentee and telephone bids; irrevocable bids are signed; by sale day 40–50% of some sales are already committed.'],
['The sale','The auctioneer opens below the reserve (chandelier bidding), takes bids from the room, phones and online, calls fair warning and drops the hammer. The buyer’s premium is added.'],
['Settlement','The buyer pays within about 30–35 days; the seller is paid roughly 35 days after the sale; the house carries the credit risk in between.'],
['After the sale','Bought-in lots are offered privately at a discount; records are claimed; the price enters the databases that set the next estimate.']
];

